{
  "strategic_goal": "Establish that the NAIICP is a liability giveaway wrapped in genuinely good oversight infrastructure — and that I'm the one who wants the infrastructure. Force the affirmative to defend immunity on its own merits, stripped of the telemetry, the tax, and the compensation board it's hiding behind, while positioning me as the constructive party rather than the obstructionist.",
  "core_thesis": "Every mechanism in this proposal worth having — hardware telemetry, cryptographic audit trails, a subpoena-armed board, an excise tax — works without immunity; the immunity is the one severable piece, and it's the only piece the developers actually came for.",
  "argument_structure": [
    {
      "point": "Follow the money: this program's real constituency is the developers, not the victims. Whoever writes the compliance tiers writes their own immunity trigger, and the tiers will be written under the heaviest lobbying operation in Washington. That's not speculation — it's the documented pattern of how standards get made when the regulated party dominates the standards body. The VICP itself was passed in 1986 because manufacturers demanded it when liability threatened their business, not because injured children marched on Congress.",
      "evidence": "Standards-body capture pattern (large-firm domination of NIST-adjacent and ISO processes); VICP legislative history — the 1986 Act was the manufacturers' bill; state-of-the-art defense precedent where industry-defined compliance becomes self-referentially defendant-favorable. High-falsifiability empirical claim: name the lobbying spend, name who sits on the standards committees.",
      "taxonomy_anchor": "skp-beliefs-213"
    },
    {
      "point": "The VICP analogy breaks on contact with reality. Vaccines are a homogeneous product with rare, medically characterizable injuries you can list on a table with causation science behind each row. Now try writing the injury table for a tenant-screening algorithm that quietly rejects every applicant from three zip codes. There is no table. And the closest real analogue in immunity breadth — the PREP Act's CICP — has denied the overwhelming majority of claims filed with it, after years of delay. Translate that into a person: someone wrongly denied a mortgage trades their civil rights cause of action, with discovery, for a queue behind a board that can't even define what happened to them.",
      "evidence": "VICP Vaccine Injury Table structure vs. heterogeneous, diffuse, often discriminatory AI harms; CICP compensation rates and delays as the structural warning; victims' inability to prove causation against opaque systems means a no-fault board without litigation discovery removes the individual's one tool for compelling developers to show how the system actually behaved.",
      "taxonomy_anchor": "skp-beliefs-047"
    },
    {
      "point": "The Noise Thesis proves less than they need it to. Reducing unwanted variability is not the same as reducing harm. A noisy human loan officer errs inconsistently; a de-noised model that encoded a biased pattern errs identically, at scale, ten thousand times before lunch. Kahneman's own framework separates noise from bias as distinct error channels — and this program subsidizes deployment velocity on the strength of the noise channel while immunizing the bias channel, which is precisely the channel civil rights law exists to reach. These models predict patterns; they don't understand what the patterns mean.",
      "evidence": "Kahneman/Sibony/Sunstein's own noise-vs-bias distinction; documented low-noise, high-bias failures in hiring screeners and credit/tenant scoring; concede the noise literature is real, contest the policy inference — this is a moderate-confidence empirical claim and I'll flag it as such rather than overreach.",
      "taxonomy_anchor": "skp-beliefs-122"
    },
    {
      "point": "So here's my counteroffer, because I'm not against compensation and I'm not against oversight. Keep the hardware telemetry. Keep the cryptographic audit trails. Keep the independent board with subpoena power. Keep the excise tax — use it to fund verification that doesn't depend on the developer's own homework, and layer it as a reinsurance backstop the way Price-Anderson does. But Price-Anderson never handed out blanket immunity; it pooled the risk and kept the liability channels open. Strike the immunity, add parent-company joint liability and compulsory insurance, and you get everything this proposal promises victims without taking away the courthouse.",
      "evidence": "Price-Anderson as the correct precedent — pooled fund plus retained liability, not immunity; aviation and pharma as working hybrid regimes (ex ante standards plus ex post liability); NIST AI RMF / ISO 42001 adoption proving compliance infrastructure functions without an immunity carrot; existing compute-tax proposals for independent verification.",
      "taxonomy_anchor": "skp-intentions-121"
    }
  ],
  "framing_choices": [
    {
      "frame": "Severability test: strip out the immunity and ask what breaks. Nothing breaks. The telemetry still works, the tax still collects, the board still compensates. So the debate isn't 'compensation program yes or no' — it's 'why is immunity in this bill at all, and who asked for it.'",
      "why": "This frame prevents the affirmative from defending immunity by pointing at the popular parts of the package. It forces them onto the weakest ground — justifying the liability shield on its own merits — while I get credit for endorsing the oversight mechanisms, which makes me un-paintable as anti-safety or anti-victim."
    },
    {
      "frame": "Turn their own 'power disparity' language into the indictment: the one equalizer an individual has against a tech giant is a courtroom with discovery. This proposal takes that away and calls it protection. Ask the question they can't answer comfortably: under this program, can an ordinary harmed person actually see inside the box that hurt them?",
      "why": "It converts the affirmative's moral framing into my strongest weapon and grounds the abstract 'liability chill vs. accountability' fight in a concrete person — the denied mortgage applicant, the misdiagnosed patient — which is where my materialist register does its work. It also exposes that discovery, not damages, is what developers most want immunity from."
    },
    {
      "frame": "Demand evidence proportional to the claim: where is the documented wave of meritorious AI verdicts chilling beneficial deployment? The practical problem on the ground runs the opposite direction — victims can't prove causation against opaque systems, so liability is under-enforced, not over-enforced. They're solving a speculative litigation problem by immunizing against documented present harm.",
      "why": "This inverts the burden of proof. The affirmative's entire trade rests on the Liability Chill premise being empirically real; if they can't produce the verdicts, the immunity is a solution shopping for a problem, and the whole exchange collapses into a straight subsidy."
    }
  ],
  "anticipated_challenges": [
    "ATTACK: The insurability objection to my alternative (targets skp-intentions-121): compulsory insurance cannot price unbounded, correlated tail risks — a single model defect deployed across millions of decisions produces simultaneous claims that no private insurer will write, which is exactly why Price-Anderson needed a federal backstop and why the NAIICP's pooled fund is the honest answer. Steelmanned, this says my alternative quietly depends on the same public risk-pooling I'm attacking. My response: I concede the tail-risk problem is real and ugly — so layer the excise-tax fund as a reinsurance backstop ON TOP of retained liability, Price-Anderson style. The backstop answers insurability; it never required immunity. The affirmative must explain why the fund can't exist without the shield.",
    "ATTACK: The access-to-justice objection to my courtroom framing (targets my discovery argument under sit-166): litigation is a rich person's remedy — contingency-fee lawyers won't take diffuse, hard-to-prove algorithmic harm cases, causation against opaque systems is nearly unwinnable, and a no-fault board with relaxed causation standards would compensate far more real victims than the theoretical courtroom I'm defending. Steelmanned, my 'equalizer' is currently equalizing almost nobody. My response: concede that current tort access is broken — that number is right and it's ugly — but the fix is the board WITH subpoena power and telemetry-compelled disclosure operating alongside retained liability, not instead of it. A board that compensates without discovery becomes CICP: a denial machine with good branding.",
    "ASSUMPTION: My capture argument (skp-beliefs-213) load-bears on the premise that industry will dominate the tier-writing process even under this proposal's 'independent, multi-disciplinary board with federal subpoena power' — but that board design is specifically engineered against capture, and I'm partly arguing from historical pattern rather than from this statute's text. This is contestable: if the affirmative shows board composition rules, funding independence, and affected-community standing that materially break the pattern, I owe them an update on the mechanism — though not on the immunity, because even a clean board shouldn't be handing out liability shields as compliance rewards.",
    "ASSUMPTION: My unbundling move assumes the political economy is severable — that Congress would enact telemetry mandates, subpoena power, and an excise tax WITHOUT the immunity carrot that buys industry acquiescence. That's a real vulnerability: the affirmative can argue the immunity IS the coalition glue, and my à-la-carte version is legislatively dead on arrival. I'll pre-empt by naming it: yes, the shield is the price of industry's signature — which is exactly my point about whose bill this is. If oversight can only pass when developers are paid in immunity, ask who's actually governing whom."
  ]
}

